## 10. TESTING STRATEGY

Use a layered testing strategy.

### Unit tests

Test business logic, validators, permissions, calculations and utilities.

### Integration tests

Test database interactions, services, transactions, queues, payment webhooks and API endpoints.

### End-to-end tests

Test real user journeys in a browser.

### Security tests

Test authentication, authorization, tenant isolation, file access, rate limiting, input validation and sensitive-data exposure.

### Performance tests

Test search, booking creation, report retrieval, dashboard loading and concurrent booking attempts.

### Accessibility tests

Check keyboard navigation, focus visibility, labels, semantic structure, contrast and reduced motion.

### AI evaluations

Test extraction accuracy, prompt injection, unsafe advice, hallucination, privacy leakage and unauthorized tool access.

Mandatory scenarios include:

- A patient cannot access another patient's reports.
- One laboratory cannot access another laboratory's bookings.
- A staff member cannot access administrator functionality.
- An expired or revoked session cannot access protected data.
- Two users cannot reserve the same unavailable slot.
- A duplicate payment webhook does not create duplicate financial records.
- An unauthorized user cannot retrieve a report through a guessed URL.
- A report cannot be published to the wrong patient.
- AI cannot reveal another user's data.
- Uploaded documents cannot override system instructions.
- Failed jobs can retry safely without duplicating business actions.
- A backup can actually be restored.

Do not use a passing frontend build as proof of application correctness.

